<!-- ── §01 ── -->
<section id="s-cliff" data-code="cliff">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Heuristic Cliff</div>
<h2>Why Confidence Thresholds Break Down</h2>

<p>
  Diffusion language models (dLLMs) generate text by starting with a fully-masked sequence and
  iteratively <em>unmasking</em> tokens over multiple steps. Unlike autoregressive models that
  produce one token at a time left-to-right, dLLMs can unmask many tokens per step — unlocking
  truly parallel generation. But which tokens to unmask, and when?
</p>
<p>
  The dominant heuristic is <strong>confidence thresholding</strong>: unmask any token whose
  predicted probability exceeds a threshold λ. Fast-dLLM (Wu et al., 2025) showed this works
  remarkably well — but only when generation proceeds in small <em>semi-autoregressive blocks</em>
  (e.g., 32 tokens at a time). Expand to full-sequence generation and confidence thresholding
  falls off a cliff.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">accuracy vs. sampling steps — LLaDA-8B on GSM8K</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">toggle generation mode</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="mode-btns" id="cliff-mode-btns">
      <button class="mode-btn active" data-mode="semi">Semi-AR (BL=32)</button>
      <button class="mode-btn" data-mode="full">Full-Diffusion (BL=256)</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="cliff-canvas" style="width:100%;height:260px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:1.25rem;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:.75rem">
      <span style="font-size:.68rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)"><span style="color:rgba(107,114,128,.7)">■</span> Random</span>
      <span style="font-size:.68rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)"><span style="color:#06b6d4">■</span> High-Confidence</span>
      <span style="font-size:.68rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)"><span style="color:#f59e0b">■</span> Fast-dLLM</span>
      <span style="font-size:.68rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)"><span style="color:#8b5cf6">■</span> RL Policy (ours)</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  Toggle between modes above. In <strong>semi-AR mode</strong>, confidence thresholding (Fast-dLLM)
  works well and the RL policy roughly matches it. In <strong>full-diffusion mode</strong>, heuristics
  stagnate or degrade while the RL policy continues improving. The RL policy achieves ~50% accuracy
  at just 12 sampling steps — roughly double what any heuristic achieves in full-diffusion mode.
</p>
<p>
  Why does this happen? Confidence heuristics are stateless: they apply the same threshold λ to
  every token in every position, every step. In full-diffusion mode, positions are correlated across
  the whole sequence — the "right" unmasking order is context-dependent in a way a single scalar
  threshold cannot capture. LLaDA in particular produces corrupted confidence scores on padding
  positions, causing Fast-dLLM to unmask tokens in a backwards (right-to-left) order.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ── §02 ── -->
<section id="s-mdm" data-code="mdm">
<div class="section-no">02 — Masked Diffusion</div>
<h2>How Diffusion LLMs Generate Text</h2>

<p>
  Before seeing how RL fixes unmasking, let's understand the generation process itself.
  A masked diffusion model (MDM) learns to <em>reverse</em> a masking process. During
  training, tokens are randomly replaced with <code>[MASK]</code> tokens; the model
  learns to predict the originals. At generation time, the process runs in reverse:
  start fully masked, iteratively unmask.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">progressive unmasking — "If each of 24 students reads 3 books…"</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">step through the unmasking process</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="token-row" id="token-display"></div>
    <div class="unmask-controls">
      <button id="unmask-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
      <button id="unmask-next">Next →</button>
      <button id="unmask-reset">Reset</button>
      <span class="step-indicator" id="unmask-step-label">Step 0 of 5</span>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:.7rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0;text-align:center">
      <span style="color:var(--muted)">■</span> masked &nbsp;
      <span style="color:var(--amber)">■</span> being unmasked &nbsp;
      <span style="color:var(--purple-l)">■</span> revealed &nbsp;
      <span style="color:var(--cyan)">■</span> prompt (frozen)
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  At each step, the model computes a distribution over all possible tokens for every masked
  position. The key decision: <strong>which subset of masked positions to actually unmask</strong>.
  Random unmasking works but ignores structure. Confidence-based methods pick high-confidence
  positions — a reasonable heuristic, but one that struggles when applied globally across a
  long sequence.
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">MDM generation step</div>
  <span class="math-var">c<sub>t</sub><sup>k</sup></span> = max<sub>v</sub> p<sub>θ</sub>(y<sub>0</sub><sup>k</sup> = v | x, y<sub>t</sub>)
  <span class="math-comment">&nbsp;&nbsp;← confidence for position k at step t</span><br>
  <span class="math-var">U<sub>t</sub><sup>λ</sup></span> = &#123;k ∈ M<sub>t</sub> | c<sub>t</sub><sup>k</sup> &gt; λ&#125;
  <span class="math-comment">&nbsp;&nbsp;← Fast-dLLM: unmask all positions above threshold</span><br>
  <span class="math-var">U<sub>t</sub><sup>π</sup></span> = &#123;k ∈ M<sub>t</sub> | u<sub>t</sub><sup>k</sup> = 1&#125;
  <span class="math-comment">&nbsp;&nbsp;← RL policy: learned adaptive decision per position</span>
</div>

<p>
  The number of function evaluations (NFEs) measures generation speed — each NFE is one
  full forward pass through the (large) dLLM. Fewer NFEs = faster generation, but potentially
  lower quality if unmasking decisions are poor.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ── §03 ── -->
<section id="s-mdp" data-code="mdp">
<div class="section-no">03 — Sampling as MDP</div>
<h2>Formalizing Unmasking as a Decision Problem</h2>

<p>
  The key insight in this paper is that dLLM sampling <em>is</em> a sequential decision-making
  problem — exactly what reinforcement learning is designed to solve. Instead of designing a
  heuristic, formalize the problem as a Markov Decision Process (MDP) and train a policy.
</p>

<svg viewBox="0 0 640 300" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="width:100%;max-width:640px;display:block;margin:1.75rem auto;background:var(--card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:10px;padding:1rem">
