<p>
  The MDP components are clean and natural:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>State</strong>: the partially-masked sequence y<sub>t</sub> (plus the prompt x)</li>
  <li><strong>Action</strong>: a binary vector u<sub>t</sub> ∈ &#123;0,1&#125;<sup>L</sup> indicating which positions to unmask</li>
  <li><strong>Transition</strong>: the dLLM samples tokens for unmasked positions (standard diffusion step)</li>
  <li><strong>Reward</strong>: given at the final step — correctness multiplied by an efficiency bonus</li>
</ul>
<p>
  The dLLM is frozen — it is the <em>environment</em>, not the policy. The policy is a tiny
  separate network that operates on top of the frozen model. This is a crucial design choice:
  it keeps the RL problem tractable and the policy transferable across models.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ── §04 ── -->
<section id="s-policy" data-code="policy">
<div class="section-no">04 — The Lightweight Policy</div>
<h2>A 300K-Parameter Policy on Confidence Scores</h2>

<p>
  The policy network is deliberately minimal. Rather than operating on token embeddings (which
  would require the same vocabulary and dimensions as the dLLM), it operates on <em>confidence
  scores</em> — the maximum predicted probability for each masked position. These are already
  computed during the dLLM forward pass, so the overhead is negligible.
</p>

<div class="step-list" id="policy-steps">
  <div class="step-box active">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Confidence Input</div>
      <div class="step-desc">For each masked position k, compute confidence c<sub>k</sub> = max<sub>v</sub> p<sub>θ</sub>(v | x, y<sub>t</sub>). High confidence ≈ model is sure; low confidence ≈ context unclear.</div>
      <div class="step-code">c = [0.92, 0.08, 0.71, 0.15, 0.88, 0.31]  # 6 masked positions</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Single-Layer Transformer</div>
      <div class="step-desc">A single transformer layer maps confidences + mask indicator + timestep → per-position logits. Self-attention lets the policy see relationships between token confidence levels.</div>
      <div class="step-code">b = transformer(c, mask_indicator, t)  # ~300K params total</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Bernoulli Unmasking</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Each position gets an independent unmasking probability s<sub>k</sub> = σ(b<sub>k</sub> / τ<sub>π</sub>). Temperature τ<sub>π</sub> controls decisiveness — lower = more selective.</div>
      <div class="step-code">s = sigmoid(b / tau_pi)  # per-position probabilities
u = Bernoulli(s).sample()  # {0,1} unmasking decisions</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">4</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Test-Time β Scaling</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Multiply unmasking probabilities by β &gt; 1 to go faster (unmask more per step), β &lt; 1 to go slower. A single trained policy covers the full accuracy-speed frontier without retraining.</div>
      <div class="step-code">u = Bernoulli(min(1, beta * s)).sample()  # β∈(0,∞) → speed knob</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="policy-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
  <button id="policy-next">Next →</button>
  <span id="policy-prog" style="font-size:.68rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">Step 1 of 4</span>
</div>

<p>
  The policy was also tested with hidden-state inputs (instead of confidence scores) —
  this required significantly larger policy models without consistent gains. The confidence
  formulation is both compact and transferable: since confidence scores are model-agnostic,
  a policy trained on LLaDA can be directly applied to Dream without retraining.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">confidence → unmask decision — interactive demo</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">drag the α slider</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted2);margin-bottom:.75rem">
      Tune <strong style="color:var(--purple)">α</strong> to explore the speed-accuracy trade-off.
      Higher α = more aggressive (unmask high-confidence tokens faster).
    </p>
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:1rem;margin-bottom:1rem">
      <span style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">α</span>
      <input type="range" id="alpha-slider" min="0" max="10" step="1" value="1"
             style="flex:1;accent-color:var(--purple)">
      <span id="alpha-val" style="font-size:.78rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--purple-l);min-width:30px">1</span>
    </div>
    <canvas id="conf-canvas" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block"></canvas>
    <p style="font-size:.7rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.5rem;margin-bottom:0">
      Bar height = confidence; purple = selected for unmasking; grey = skip this step.
      Approximate visualization of the policy's behavior at different α values.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ── §05 ── -->
<section id="s-train" data-code="train">
<div class="section-no">05 — Training with GRPO</div>
<h2>Reinforcement Learning from Scratch</h2>

<p>
  The policy is trained from scratch using <strong>Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO)</strong>,
  the same RL algorithm that powers reasoning models like DeepSeek-R1. For each math problem,
  the policy samples G=8 different unmasking trajectories and compares their rewards — no
  reference model or value network needed.
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Multiplicative Reward — the key design choice</div>
  R(y, y<sub>T̂</sub>) = r(y, y<sub>T̂</sub>) × <span class="math-var">(1 − (T−T̂)/T)<sup>α</sup></span>
  <span class="math-comment">&nbsp;&nbsp;← correctness × efficiency bonus</span><br>
  <span class="math-comment">&nbsp;where r = 1 if answer is correct, 0 otherwise</span><br>
  <span class="math-comment">&nbsp;T = max steps, T̂ = steps actually used</span>
</div>

<p>
  The <strong>multiplicative</strong> reward is critical. An additive alternative — r − α·(steps/T) —
  caused reward hacking: early in training when all samples are wrong, faster-but-wrong policies
  got positive advantages, collapsing to "unmask everything at once." The multiplicative form
  ensures no reward unless the answer is <em>correct</em>, making it impossible to game by
  being fast.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">training dynamics — correctness vs. training step, GSM8K</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">select α value</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="mode-btns" id="alpha-mode-btns">
      <button class="mode-btn active" data-alpha="0">α=0</button>
      <button class="mode-btn" data-alpha="0.3">α=0.3</button>
      <button class="mode-btn" data-alpha="1">α=1</button>
      <button class="mode-btn" data-alpha="3">α=3</button>
      <button class="mode-btn" data-alpha="10">α=10</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="train-canvas" style="width:100%;height:220px;display:block"></canvas>
    <p style="font-size:.7rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.5rem;margin-bottom:0">
      Higher α → faster policy but less accurate. α=10 shows training instability (reward too steep).
      The speed-accuracy frontier is navigated by changing α at train time, or β at test time.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The policies are trained on a mixture of GSM8K and MATH training data (~15,000 samples),
  completing training in roughly 800 steps on a single GPU. Each training step rolls out G=8
  trajectories per prompt, making the effective sample efficiency much higher than naive
  policy gradient methods.
</p>
<p>
  A notable finding: policies trained without expert steering learn to allocate compute
  <em>uniformly</em> across blocks, while Fast-dLLM front-loads compute into earlier blocks.
  The RL policy with α=10 learns an interesting specialization: slow down on the very last
  block when generating numerical answers — adapting its compute allocation to the structure
  of the generation task.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ── §06 ── -->
<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">06 — Results</div>
<h2>Full-Diffusion Without the Penalty</h2>

<p>
  The headline result: in full-diffusion mode (BL=256), RL policies achieve roughly <strong>50%
  accuracy at just 12 steps</strong> — approximately double all heuristic baselines regardless
  of whether those baselines use semi-AR generation or not. With expert steering (using Fast-dLLM
  samples as exploration), RL closes the gap to semi-AR performance at mid-to-high NFE counts.
</p>

<h3>Full-Diffusion (BL=256) — GSM8K at ~12 NFEs</h3>
